WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.832
Next, we introduce regular expressions.

00:00:02.832 --> 00:00:08.997
A regular expression, often called regex, is a way to describe a pattern in text.

00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:14.657
Regex can look intimidating at first because the syntax uses special symbols.

00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:18.542
But we can start with small patterns.

00:00:18.542 --> 00:00:25.927
In this example, the pattern checks whether an ID has two capital letters, a dash, and four digits.

00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:30.667
The caret symbol means the start of the text.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.742
The dollar sign means the end of the text.

00:00:32.742 --> 00:00:36.537
The bracket A through Z means a capital letter.

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:40.932
The curly braces with two means exactly two capital letters.

00:00:40.932 --> 00:00:43.302
Then we have a dash.

00:00:43.302 --> 00:00:50.387
The backslash d means a digit, and curly braces with four means exactly four digits.

00:00:50.387 --> 00:00:53.627
Run the cell and look at which IDs match.

00:00:53.627 --> 00:00:56.822
AB dash 1204 should match.

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:59.402
NC dash 2026 should also match.

00:00:59.402 --> 00:01:03.807
But A dash 1204 has only one letter, and xy dash 9999 uses lowercase letters,

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:05.672
so those do not match.

00:01:05.672 --> 00:01:12.742
Regex is powerful, but the best way to learn it is to test small examples and build the

00:01:12.742 --> 00:01:14.391
pattern gradually.
